{"item_id":"E-OPT-404","title":"Check the process before you automate it","body":"Before you build a regression suite around a workflow, first decide whether the workflow itself is stable enough to deserve one. For a small business owner, that usually means one repeatable task: customer questions, invoices, or data work. The real question is not “can AI do this?” but “does this process already behave the same way often enough that automation would be checking something, not guessing everything?”

If the manual process changes from week to week, automation will only repeat those differences faster. That is not a failure of software; it is a sign the process still needs cleanup. The useful boundary is simple: can you describe the steps clearly enough that someone else would follow them and reach the same result most of the time?

A practical next step is to document one workflow on a single page: trigger, inputs, steps, edge cases, and the point where a human must still check the work. That gives you a real basis for deciding whether to keep it manual, standardise it first, or automate part of it later. Bareni’s diagnostic approach starts there: identify one recurring workflow and be honest about whether it is ready for automation, rather than asking software to guess the missing pieces."}

